      "source_text": "A formal object <MATH_0001> of <MATH_0002> consists of a Noetherian\ncomplete local <MATH_0003>-algebra <MATH_0004> whose residue field is of finite type\nover <MATH_0005>, together with elements <MATH_0006>\nfor all <MATH_0007> such that <MATH_0008>.\nBy the discussion in the proof of Lemma <REF_0001>\nwe see that either <MATH_0009> is a formal object of <MATH_0010> or a formal\nobject of <MATH_0011>. In the first case we see that <MATH_0012> is effective\nby Lemma <REF_0002>. The second case is the interesting case.\nSet <MATH_0013>. We will construct an element\n<MATH_0014> whose image in <MATH_0015>\nis <MATH_0016> for all <MATH_0017>.\n\n<STRUCT_0001><STRUCT_0002>\nWe may view the collection of elements <MATH_0018> as a morphism\n<MATH_0019>\nof locally Noetherian formal algebraic spaces over <MATH_0020>. Observe that <MATH_0021>\nis <TEXTITOPEN_0001>not<TEXTITCLOSE_0001> an adic morphism in general. To fix this, let <MATH_0022>\nbe the ideal corresponding to the formal closed subspace\n<MATH_0023>\nNote that <MATH_0024>. Set <MATH_0025>.\nSince <MATH_0026> is <MATH_0027>-adically complete it is a fortiori\n<MATH_0028>-adically complete (Algebra, Lemma <REF_0003>).\nMoreover, we claim that for each <MATH_0029> the morphism\n<MATH_0030>\nactually comes from a morphism\n<MATH_0031>\nNamely, this follows from writing <MATH_0032> as in the\nproof of Lemma <REF_0004>, noticing that <MATH_0033>\nmaps into <MATH_0034>, and applying Formal Spaces, Lemma\n<REF_0005>\nto algebraize this to a morphism <MATH_0035>\nas desired. Let us denote <MATH_0036> the formal spectrum\nof <MATH_0037> endowed with the <MATH_0038>-adic topology -- equivalently the formal\ncompletion of <MATH_0039> along <MATH_0040>. Using the morphisms\n<MATH_0041> we obtain an adic morphism\n<MATH_0042>\nof locally Noetherian formal algebraic spaces over <MATH_0043>.\nConsider the base change\n<MATH_0044>\nThis is a formal modification by\nAlgebraization of Formal Spaces, Lemma\n<REF_0006>.\nHence by the main theorem on dilatations\n(Algebraization of Formal Spaces, Theorem <REF_0007>)\nwe obtain a proper morphism\n<MATH_0045>\nwhich is an isomorphism over <MATH_0046> and\nwhose completion recovers the formal modification above, in other words\n<MATH_0047>\nThis in particular tells us we have a compatible system of morphisms\n<MATH_0048>\nHence by Grothendieck's algebraization theorem (in the form of\nMore on Morphisms of Spaces, Lemma\n<REF_0008>)\nwe obtain a morphism\n<MATH_0049>\nover <MATH_0050> recovering the morphisms displayed above. Then finally\nsetting <MATH_0051> the restriction of <MATH_0052> to\n<MATH_0053> gives the third component of our\ndesired element <MATH_0054>.\n",
